<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teriitaria II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1790–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teriitaria II (c. 1790–1858) was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a> in the Society Islands from 1815 and a wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-ii">Pōmare II</a> of Tahiti. She supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> and led Huahine in resisting French claims during the Franco-Tahitian War of the 1840s. She was deposed by Huahine's chiefs in 1852.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teriitaria-ii.jpg" alt="Teriitaria II" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Chazal / Ambroise Tardieu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Teriitaria_Ariipaea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Teriitaria was born around 1790, the eldest child of Tamatoa III, king of Raiatea, and of Tura'iari'i Ehevahine, a daughter of Queen Tehaʻapapa I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a>. The Tamatoa line was counted among the highest-ranking chiefly families of the Society Islands, but after Puni of Faanui on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a> conquered Raiatea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahaa">Tahaa</a> in 1768, it lived on Huahine. Titles passed through first-born daughters, so her place as eldest daughter gave her special status.</p><p>Around 1809 her father married her and her younger sister Teriʻitoʻoterai Teremoemoe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-ii">Pōmare II</a> of Tahiti, then in exile on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moorea">Moorea</a> and in need of allies. He preferred the younger sister, but Teriitaria received the rank of queen and the title Pōmare Vahine. She had no children; her sister bore the future Pōmare III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-iv">Pōmare IV</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Teriitaria took the Christian side and led warriors herself in the wars that restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-ii">Pōmare II</a>. On 11 November 1815 she stood at the head of the Christian warriors beside Mahine, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maiao">Maiao</a>, at the Battle of Te Feipī, which restored Pōmare rule over Tahiti. After Pōmare II died in 1821 she sat on the regency council for the young Pōmare III, heading it from 1823, and in 1826 she signed a commercial treaty with Commodore Thomas ap Catesby Jones of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>She remained regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-iv">Pōmare IV</a> after 1827, but her attempts to monopolise the pearl trade and to fix prices and wages led to her replacement by Tati in April 1828. In February 1832 she helped to put down the Taiarapu rebellion, commanding her warriors with sabre and pistol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For decades she ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a> from Tahiti. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> seized the Tahitian throne she went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-iv">Pōmare IV</a> into exile on Raiatea from 1844 to 1847. On 17 January 1846 Commandant Louis Adolphe Bonard landed 400 soldiers and marines at Fare; at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeva">Maeva</a> her forces held them off for two days, killing 18 and wounding 43, and the French withdrew.</p><p>The people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a> later accused her of seizing land, punishing without regard to her own laws and oppressing foreigners, and replaced her with Ari'imate Teurura'i. Her supporters were defeated in a civil war on 18 March 1854, and she was taken to Tahiti. She died at the royal palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a> in 1858.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teriitaria II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Huahine's ruling family was closely tied by marriage to the Kingdom of Tahiti; <b>Teriitaria II</b>, queen from 1815, was also a wife of Pōmare II.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-huahine">Kingdom of Huahine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Raiatea. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-16.82,-151.44&amp;year=1834" aria-label="Raiatea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="430 2788 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Teriitaria II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M880 3058h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M880 3058h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-16.82,-151.44&amp;year=1834">What was here in 1834? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
